## Generated by fetch.sh (no network)
- scanned.pdf (no byte hash; content-checked by fetch.sh): 5 image-only pages, no text layer. standard-form.pdf rasterised by `pdftoppm -r 100 -gray -jpeg -jpegopt quality=75` (850x1100 px per page), each JPEG wrapped as a DCTDecode image on a 612x792 page by a stdlib-Python writer inside fetch.sh (our CLI has no image->PDF command). Excluded from SHA256SUMS because the pixels depend on the host's poppler rasteriser and libjpeg (reference value on poppler 24.04.0: 1e2b2150ec14d90ba05f36d21d5665c81639d6d886905e2919ca2515c1755705, informational only). fetch.sh checks instead: 5 pages, pdftotext empty, exactly one 850x1100 image per page (pdfimages -list), qpdf --check ok.
- encrypted.pdf (no byte hash; content-checked by fetch.sh): standard-form.pdf encrypted by `qpdf --encrypt --bits=256` (AES-256, R6), user password `lpdf-test`, owner password `lpdf-owner`, 5 pages. Not byte-reproducible: qpdf draws a random R6 file key, salts and /Perms for every run, and no qpdf option fixes them. Excluded from SHA256SUMS; fetch.sh checks instead: R = 6, opens with the user password, 5 pages, and its pdftotext output equals standard-form.pdf's (host-independent: both sides use the same local poppler).
